			Driver installation on NT
			-------------------------

The driver installation procedure is not yet integrated with that used
for other Aculab products. Instead, separate programs are provided
for installation and removal of the driver.

File names used in this document are relative to the $(TiNG)/test
directory.


1) Plugging in the card

There is no special preparation required before plugging in a PCI or
Compact PCI card.

If you are installing a Prosody ISA card (the S2 card) or a Basic Rate
card (the BR8 card), it is probably best to first install the standard
Prosody driver (as described in the document "Prosody ISA Card
Installation Instructions" (file S2INST.PDF) or "Basic Rate Card Prosody
Module Installation Instructions" (file BRINST.PDF) depending on which
card is being used - these documents are available from
http://www.aculab.com in the Acrobat PDF archive).  This ensures that
there are no conflicts. Alternatively, you can check for a free I/O area
using the "Windows NT diagnostics" program ("Resources" tab, "I/O Port"
button), set the card for the appropriate base address, and plug
it in (with the machine switched off of course!).

If the standard Aculab speech processing driver is installed, you need
to stop it. However if you don't know what I/O address an ISA card has
been set to, use "Windows NT diagnostics" to find out what resources are
assigned to "AculabSpeech" as this is easier than getting the card out
of the machine and working it out from the switches.

To stop the standard driver from "Control Panel" run "Devices"
select "AculabSpeech", Select "Stop" button, select "Startup" button,
and set it to "Manual".



2) Configuration

Edit the file TiNGpar with any text editor (e.g. notepad) and
change any parameters as necessary. The format of this file is farily
obvious from the supplied example, but an exact description is also
provided as follows.

Each line in this file is interpreted separately, though
lines which configure a single card are kept together to make it
easier to understand.

Blank lines and lines starting with a hash (#) symbol are ignored.

All other lines consist of a value and a key. The value can be in
decimal (the default) or hex (if it starts with "0x"). The keys all
start with a prefix, which specifies which card is being configured,
and the name of the key. For example, the line:

	0x354	S2Card0\ISAIOBaseAddress0

configures the I/O address of the first S2 card to be 0x354 (decimal
852). To configure the second card, the prefix would be "S2Card1".
The third would be "S2Card2" etc. 

The types of card which are currently possible are:
	S2Card0		the Prosody ISA S2 card
	BR8Card0	the Basic Rate card (4 or 8 lines with Prosody)
	P1Card0		the Prosody PCI P1 or CompactPCI C1 card

The values which must be configured are:
	S2Card0\ISAIoBaseaddress0

	BR8Card0\ISAIoBaseaddress
	BR8Card0\ISAInterruptNumber
	BR8Card0\ISASharedPhysicalAddress
	BR8Card0\CardInterruptTerminatsion
	BR8Card0\SwitchCardNumber

	P1Card0\SwitchCardNumber

These are explained below:

S2Card0\ISAIOBaseAddress0
	This is the I/O address of the card. It must also be set on
	the card by setting the DIP switches appropriately. This is
	the value which would be used with the "-p" option on the
	standard Prosody driver.

S2Card0\ISAExclusivePhysicalAddress
	This is the address for the memory window. The standard
	driver configures this with the "-x" option. If this value is
	omitted (or specified as zero) the driver will ask NT for a
	suitable area of memory, so it is not normally necessary to
	set this value except to avoid a conflict with another
	device. Note that a shared memory window is not currently
	possible.

S2Card0\ISAInterruptNumber
	This specifies the interrupt to use. The standard
	driver configures this with the "-i" option. If this value is
	omitted (or specified as zero) the driver will ask NT for a
	suitable interrupt number, so it is not normally necessary to
	set this value except to avoid a conflict with another device
	or to force an interrupt to be shared with another device.

S2Card0\S2CardInterruptTermination
	This value must be '1' unless this card shares an interrupt
	with any other cards, in which case exactly one of the cards
	must set it to '1' and the rest must set it to '0'. If the
	interrupt is automatically assigned it is not shared, so this
	value must be specifed as '1'.


BR8Card0\ISAIOBaseAddress
	This is the I/O address of the card. It must also be set on
	the card by setting the DIP switches appropriately. This is
	the value which would be used with the "-p" option on the
	standard Prosody driver.

BR8Card0\ISASharedPhysicalAddress
	This is the address for the memory window. The standard
	driver configures this with the "-w" option. Note that the shared
	memory window must be the same as is configured for the call
	control driver which controls this card.

BR8Card0\ISAInterruptNumber
	This is the interrupt number to use. The standard
	driver configures this with the "-i" option. Note that this
	value must be the same as is configured for the call
	control driver which controls this card.

BR8Card0\CardInterruptTermination
	This value must be '1' unless this card shares an interrupt
	with any other cards, in which case exactly one of the cards
	must set it to '1' and the rest must set it to '0'.

BR8Card0\SwitchCardNumber
	This value is used to calculate the value that
	sm_channel_info() uses when returning the number of the card for
	passing to te switch driver. The standard driver configures an
	equivalent value with the "-p" option.


P1Card0\SwitchCardNumber
	This value is used to calculate the value that
	sm_channel_info() uses when returning the number of the card for
	passing to te switch driver. The standard driver configures an
	equivalent value with the "-p" option.
	Note that the name of this key will soon change to incorporate
	the card serial number, because it is not possible to guarantee
	in what order PCI cards will be enumerated.


Note that for the S2 card by default the driver asks the NT operating
system to allocate the IRQ number, DMA channel, and memory range and
these only need to be manually specified when NT allocates values which
cannot be used (e.g. on some machines the BIOS disables these resources
and NT doesn't know that they are not usable).



3) Installing and starting the driver

Run startacu.bat. If you get an error message:
	StartService error (487): Attempt to access invalid address
then the IO address specified in the TiNGpar file is already in use
(possibly by the standard Aculab speech processing driver).

This uses the installation program ../util/gen-WINNT/drvinst which
can install an arbitrary driver.



4) Stopping and removing the driver 

Run stopacu.bat.

This uses the driver removal program ../util/gen-WINNT/servdel which 
can be used to delete any NT driver or service by
specifying its name.
